.TH LOGIND 8
.SH NAME
logind \- login daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/logind
[
.B -1
] [
.BI -k " keydb"
] [
.BI -s " signerkey"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Logind
is normally started by
.IR svc (8)
to service requests on the
.B inflogin
TCP/IP port,
to provide a `signing' service (identity authentication) for a network.
Ultimately, the client receives a certificate that can be used to establish its identity
with any host that is willing to honour certificates from the certificate's signer.
.PP
The signer constructs the certificate from the contents
of the signer's key file
.BR /keydb/signerkey ,
typically created by
.IR createsignerkey (8),
and
the ID string and password supplied by the client (which are used to access
.IR keyfs (4)
to check identity).
.PP
The protocol involves an exchange of information between the client and server,
as summarised in
.IR login (6).
The client side of this exchange can be managed by the
.B Login
module; see
.IR security-login (2).
.I Logind
speaks version 2 of that protocol.
The
.B -1
option makes it serve clients that have only the original version 1 as well;
it tells the two apart by the first byte they send.
Version 1 protects the user's secret much less well and should be
allowed only while such clients remain.
.PP
The
.B -k
option names the directory of users' secrets (default:
.BR /mnt/keys ,
where
.IR keyfs (4)
is normally mounted), and
.B -s
the signer's key file (default:
.BR /keydb/signerkey ).
.I Logind
is run for each call by
.IR listen (1),
which must be given its
.B -t
option, as
.B svc/auth
does:
without it
.I listen
unmounts
.B /mnt/keys
from the name space it shares with the command (and with whoever started it),
and
.I logind
then finds no users.
An entry's secret is the SHA-1 digest of the password or a verifier (see
.IR changelogin (8));
a user with a verifier is served by version 2 only, whatever
.B -1
says.
A request for a name with no entry, or an expired one, is refused in
the same words as one with the wrong secret; the reason is written to the console.
.PP
Client and server communicate over the Secure Socket Layer device
.IR ssl (3)
once keys are agreed.
.SH FILES
.TF /keydb/signerkey
.TP
.B /keydb/keys
.TP
.B /keydb/signerkey
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/logind.b
